# Cluster config for run-agent.sh / run-agent.ps1 / run-orchestrator.sh and
# the dashboard.
#
#   cp nodes.conf.example nodes.conf
#
# You normally need ONE setting: where the orchestrator lives. Everything else
# is discovered:
#
#   - each machine detects its own LAN IP and advertises it when it registers,
#     so the orchestrator learns the topology from the nodes themselves;
#   - the agent's port defaults to 9001, which is right when a machine runs a
#     single agent (the usual case);
#   - nothing anywhere needs to know how many nodes exist, or which. Pipeline
#     roles are assigned by position among whoever has registered.
#
# So a new machine joins with just:
#
#   ./run-agent.sh NODE_ID=node-d
#   .\run-agent.ps1 NodeId=node-d        # Windows
#
# The node id only has to be unique -- it is a label, not an address.

# --- the one setting that matters -------------------------------------------
# Where the orchestrator is reachable. On the orchestrator's own machine this
# can stay as it is.
#
# It can also be given without this file, which is often easier:
#   export ORCHESTRATOR=http://192.0.2.10:9000
#   ./run-agent.sh NODE_ID=node-a ORCHESTRATOR=http://192.0.2.10:9000
#   ...or typed into the dashboard's "orchestrator url" field once.

ORCHESTRATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1
ORCHESTRATOR_PORT=9000

# --- optional overrides -----------------------------------------------------
# Only needed when auto-detection gets it wrong or you want fixed values --
# a machine with several interfaces where the wrong one is picked, or two
# agents sharing one host and so needing different ports.
#
# A node id maps to its keys by uppercasing and turning '-' into '_':
#
#   node-a     -> NODE_A_HOST     / NODE_A_PORT
#   garage-pc  -> GARAGE_PC_HOST  / GARAGE_PC_PORT
#
# No limit on how many you list, and no requirement to list any.
#
# NODE_A_HOST=192.0.2.11
# NODE_A_PORT=9001
